@command
A command that can be used for various purposes.

Commands:

@command.get.input
Retrieves keyboard input.

@command.get.input.mouse
Retrieves mouse input.

@command.get
Used to obtain a command.

(Subcommands of .get:
create
creates a variable or a object, the variable must be specified as int, string, bool, or float. 
Example:
@command.get.create.variable.int.#health)
object creation:
@command.get.create.object.ball
Creates a circular object.

@command.get.create.object.cube
Creates a cube object.

@command.affiliate.(symbol).to#
Associates a symbol with a variable.

@command.console.(type).==true or false
Determines whether the program will output console messages. It can also be used with set, although the command itself works perfectly without set.

Console types:

info
Displays informational messages.

debug
Displays information useful for debugging.

warning
Warns about things that could potentially break your code.

error
Displays runtime errors.

all
Enables every console output type, including automatic .log file creation (the filename is still customizable).

log
Creates a .log file containing every console message with timestamps.

Sub-subcommands of .log:

.file=="".log
Allows you to specify a custom filename for the generated .log file.

let
Allows variable X to be used by variable Y.

array
A list that can contain int, bool, string, or float values.

Array commands:

get.(list_name).print
Retrieves a list and prints it.

rename.(name)
Renames a list.

add
Adds a list.

remove.(name)
Removes a list.

file.(path)
Opens, reads, or writes a file. A path must always be specified.

File subcommands:

read
Reads an existing file.

write
Writes to a file.

for.(times)
Repeats the specified block the given number of times.

instruction.(priority 0-10)
Sends an instruction that the program must execute. It is intended for extremely important operations.

forever
Repeats until the program closes.

return.(variable or result)
Returns the result of an addition, subtraction, multiplication, or division involving either a variable or an integer value.

import
Imports something useful.

Imports:
(None yet XD)

if
A conditional statement.
Example:
if.#life.using.#health.set.#health.to.0

else
Executes another action if the condition in the if statement is not met.
Example:
else.set.#health.to.100

elseif
Executes another condition if the previous if condition is not met.

while
Repeats as long as a condition is true.

function
Defines a function.

Example:
function.velocityX.set.to.100

Built-in functions:
velocityX
velocityY
velocityZ

You can also create your own functions.

Example:
function.heal

Then call it with:

heal()

// Comment

*
Multiplication operator.

==
Equality operator.

+
Addition operator.

-
Subtraction operator.

/
Division operator.

>
Greater than.

<
Less than.

true
Represents the boolean value true (child of bool).

false
Represents the boolean value false (child of bool).

int
A data type used when creating variables.
Stores positive and negative whole numbers (no decimal places).

float
A data type used when creating variables.
Stores positive and negative decimal numbers.

string
A data type used when creating variables.
Represents text using letters, numbers, and symbols.

bool
A data type used when creating variables.
Represents two possible states: true and false.
Parent type of true and false.

variable("(name)")
References a variable.

#
Marks the beginning of a variable.

Example:
#money

Without the # symbol, the code ignores it.

print
Prints text.

Example:

print("Hello world")

Output:

Hello world

print.line.(delay)
Prints text one character at a time.

Example:

print.line.5.("Hello world")

Output:
The text is displayed character by character with a 5-second delay.

draw.(radius).(shape)
or

draw.(shape).(radius)

Draws geometric shapes.

Available shapes:

square

triangle

circle

end
Ends the program.

end.repeat.(times)
Ends the program after repeating the code the specified number of times.

end.wait.(seconds)
Ends the program after waiting the specified number of seconds.

send("Message")
Sends a message globally.

when
Used to specify what should happen when something occurs to a variable.

Example:

when.received("message").set.#health.to.100-1

wait.(seconds)
Waits the specified number of seconds before executing the next action.

}
Closes a block.

Blocks must be closed using a staircase-like structure.

Example:

     }
    }
   }
  }
 }
}

The following format is also valid:

     }
 }}}}
}

,
Allows a command or part of a command to continue onto another line.

You can use as many commas as needed depending on how the variable uses them.

Example:

@command.let.text,
    ,.users

@command.test,,
   ,,.users

set
Assigns a value (text or number) to something.

%
Assigns a percentage value.

use
Allows one variable to use another variable.

$
Displays the value of a variable.

It only works if the symbol has been associated with a variable.

Example:

@command.affiliate.$.to.#money

Output:

$100

The $ symbol is read-only and is intended only for displaying values.

To modify a variable's value, use:

#variableName

.
Acts as a mandatory separator (similar to a space).

Correct:

@command.get.create.variable.#test

Incorrect:

@command get create variable #test

